# Testing and benchmarking

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:stream_data, "~> 1.4"},
  {:benchee, "~> 1.5"}
])
```

## Two ways of flattening

This notebook writes a function in two ways, checks both with a property test, then benchmarks them. The function follows each number of a list with its square, so `[1, 2, 3]` becomes `[1, 1, 2, 4, 3, 9]`. For benchmarking, it's important that the functions are within a module - otherwise they would be interpreted (instead of compiled) and perform worse.

```elixir
defmodule Flatten do
  def flat_map(list), do: Enum.flat_map(list, &[&1, &1 * &1])
  def map_flatten(list), do: list |> Enum.map(&[&1, &1 * &1]) |> List.flatten()
end
```

## Property tests

StreamData generates random lists, and ExUnit checks that both ways return the same, and that it's each number followed by its square.

```elixir
ExUnit.start(autorun: false)

defmodule FlattenTest do
  use ExUnit.Case
  use ExUnitProperties

  property "follows each number with its square" do
    check all list <- list_of(integer()) do
      result = Flatten.flat_map(list)
      assert Flatten.map_flatten(list) == result
      assert Enum.chunk_every(result, 2) == Enum.map(list, &[&1, &1 * &1])
    end
  end
end

ExUnit.run()
```

## Benchmarks

Benchee also tries to read the CPU and memory information by running programs like `cat`, which doesn't work in the browser, so it prints errors before the results.

```elixir
Benchee.run(
  %{
    "flat_map" => &Flatten.flat_map/1,
    "map.flatten" => &Flatten.map_flatten/1
  },
  inputs: %{"10k integers" => Enum.to_list(1..10_000)}
)

:ok
```
